 **Edge Cases and Pitfalls:**
-- **Parallel Rays:** If the ray is parallel to the triangle plane, the determinant approaches zero, causing division by zero. This is handled by the $|det| < \epsilon$ check [S4, unofficial].
-- **Degenerate Triangles:** If the triangle vertices are collinear, the area is zero and the determinant will be zero (or near zero). The parallelism check handles this case [G].
-- **Precision:** Using a small epsilon for comparisons is crucial for numerical stability, especially when the ray is nearly parallel to the triangle plane [S1, S2, unofficial].
-- **Winding Order and Back-Face Culling:** The sign of $det$ indicates the orientation of the hit relative to the ray. If $det > 0$, the ray hits the front face; if $det < 0$, it hits the back face. The algorithm computes intersections for both, but forward-facing hits typically require $t > 0$ [S3, G, unofficial].
+*   **Parallel Rays:** If $\text{det}$ is near zero, division by zero occurs. The epsilon check prevents this [S1, S2, S5, S6, unofficial].
+*   **Degenerate Triangles:** If vertices are collinear, $\mathbf{e}_1$ and $\mathbf{e}_2$ are parallel, making $\text{det}$ zero. The algorithm correctly rejects these as parallel/non-intersecting.
+*   **Back-Facing:** The algorithm returns intersections for both front and back faces. The caller must check the dot product of the triangle normal and ray direction to cull back-faces if desired [S6, unofficial].
+*   **Precision:** Using a fixed epsilon is standard, but for very large or very small scenes, relative epsilon based on triangle size may be more robust [G].
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-- **Time Complexity:** $O(1)$ per triangle. The algorithm performs a fixed number of arithmetic operations (cross products, dot products, divisions) [G].
-- **Memory Complexity:** $O(1)$. No additional memory is required beyond local variables. This is a key advantage over methods that require precomputed plane equations [S4, S5, unofficial].
-- **Performance:** It is considered one of the fastest ray-triangle intersection routines, particularly for meshes without precomputed plane equations [S5, unofficial].
-- **Strengths:** Minimal storage requirements, clean implementation, no need for plane equation computation [S5, unofficial].
-- **Limitations:** Sensitive to numerical precision in degenerate cases (parallel rays, degenerate triangles) [S1, S2, unofficial]. Does not inherently cull back faces without checking the sign of $det$ or $t$ [G].
+*   **Time Complexity:** $O(1)$ per triangle. It involves a constant number of vector operations (subtractions, cross products, dot products) and one division.
+*   **Memory Complexity:** $O(1)$ auxiliary space. No plane equation needs to be precomputed or stored, saving memory compared to plane-based methods [S7, unofficial].
+*   **Performance:** It is widely considered the fastest general-purpose ray-triangle intersection routine for meshes without precomputed plane equations [S7, unofficial].
+*   **Strengths:** Minimal storage, fast execution, numerically stable with epsilon checks.
+*   **Limitations:** Does not handle back-face culling internally; requires caller to check normal if needed. Precision issues can arise with extremely thin triangles or rays nearly parallel to the plane if epsilon is not chosen carefully.
 
 ## Background
-The Möller-Trumbore algorithm was published by Tomas Möller and Ben Trumbore in the article "Fast, Minimum Storage Ray-Triangle Intersection" (1997) [S1, S5, unofficial]. It is used in volume rendering pipelines to determine entry and exit distances (sampling intervals) for triangles [S3, unofficial]. It is referenced in implementations such as GPView [S1, unofficial].
+The algorithm was presented by Tomas Möller and Ben Trumbore in the 1997 paper "Fast, Minimum Storage Ray/Triangle Intersection" [S7, unofficial]. It was developed to provide a clean, efficient alternative to methods requiring plane equation computation, leveraging a change of basis to solve for barycentric coordinates directly. It is a fundamental building block in ray tracing and volume rendering for determining ray-primitive intersections [S1, S4, S5, unofficial].
 
